    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work when called over stdio. `pytest -q` passes (1 test).\n\n**Version note:** the pinned SDK is `mcp==2.2.0`. In 2.x the class that used to be called `FastMCP` is now `MCPServer`, imported from `mcp.server.mcpserver`, and the old `mcp.server.fastmcp` import fails on purpose. So the code uses `MCPServer(\"notes\")` with its `@server.tool()` decorator. The tool type hints give the input schemas, and the docstrings become the tool descriptions.\n\n**The unknown-title error:** `word_count` raises `ToolError`, which is the SDK's exception for an expected tool failure. The client then gets a normal result with `is_error=True` and the message, so the calling model can read why it failed. Any other exception, such as a plain `KeyError` or `ValueError`, counts as a crash. The model then sees only `Error executing tool word_count` and the \"no such note\" text is dropped.\n\n**What I checked with a real stdio client session:**\n- `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` returns `saved a`.\n- `word_count(\"a\")` returns `4`.\n- `word_count(\"zzz\")` returns `is_error=True` with the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zzz'`.\n- `python -m app.smoke` starts the server over stdio and lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n\n**One leftover:** the scratch script I used for this check, `_check.py` in the project root, is still there because deleting it needed your approval. Please delete it, or tell me to go ahead."
